INT. GRAND HOTEL - PENTHOUSE SUITE - NIGHT

Art deco. Polished brass. A fire crackles behind a screen. Through tall windows, the glittering city.

VICTOR LANGFORD (50s, dinner jacket, whiskey in hand) stands before a window, admiring his reflection. At a writing desk sits his wife IRIS LANGFORD (40s, silver gown, a jeweled pin), reading a letter she does not seem to be reading.

IRIS
You're quiet tonight, Victor.

VICTOR
I'm thinking.

IRIS
About what?

VICTOR
Everything.

IRIS
That's not an answer.

VICTOR
It's the only honest one.

She folds the letter. Slips it into her bag. He turns from the window, smiling like a man at ease.

VICTOR (CONT'D)
Shall we dine?

IRIS
Here?

VICTOR
Upstairs. Eight o'clock. A table for two.

IRIS
How romantic.

VICTOR
I try.

He crosses to her. Kisses her forehead. She does not flinch, but she does not close her eyes either.

A soft knock. Both turn.

VICTOR (CONT'D)
That will be the champagne.

He opens the door. A tall man in a waiter's jacket stands in the hall, tray in hand, a bottle sweating in an ice bucket. But his shoes are wrong, military black, and his hands are bare of the white gloves the hotel requires.

WAITER LEONARD CROSS (30s, thin smile, calm eyes) steps in and sets the tray on the table.

LEONARD
Good evening, Mr. and Mrs. Langford.

IRIS
You're new.

LEONARD
Very.

VICTOR
Where's Henri?

LEONARD
Henri has the night off.

VICTOR
Henri never has a night off.

LEONARD
He does tonight.

Silence. The fire pops. Victor's smile fades.

VICTOR
Iris, would you excuse us?

IRIS
No.

LEONARD
Mrs. Langford may stay.

VICTOR
Of course she may. It's her suite as well.

LEONARD
It is.

IRIS
(quietly)
Victor, who is he?

VICTOR
A waiter.

LEONARD
(at the same time)
A friend of Mr. Delacroix.

The name hangs in the air. Victor sets down his glass. Iris rises, one hand on the desk.

IRIS
Delacroix.

VICTOR
Iris.

LEONARD
He sends his regards.

IRIS
You told me he was dead.

VICTOR
He is.

LEONARD
Not entirely.

Victor takes a step back. Iris takes a step forward. Leonard lifts the champagne bottle, peels the foil, and eases the cork.

VICTOR
Iris, don't.

IRIS
Leonard, is it?

LEONARD
Yes.

IRIS
Tell me, Leonard, what does Mr. Delacroix want?

LEONARD
Everything, Mrs. Langford. But tonight, I think he'd settle for the truth.

The cork pops. Foam spills over the bottle.

Victor stares at it. Iris stares at Leonard. Leonard stares at no one.

LEONARD (CONT'D)
(pouring three glasses)
Shall we begin?
